# Jetpack areas and the areas each one may use (direct dependencies only; the harness adds the closure).
# Each area is checked as its own unit containing only itself and its dependency closure, so an area
# cannot reach code it did not declare. Layers mirror the Rust crates: models (L1/L2) below the engine
# (L3) below the CLI. When cross-package enums, type imports and dependency check reuse work (Tower
# #3673, #3661), each area becomes a package.jet with exactly these dependency edges.
#
# Format: `Area: Dep, Dep` (an area with no dependencies ends with `:`). Order is bottom-up.

Compiler hash:
Compiler front end: Compiler hash
Foundation: Compiler front end
TrustRoot: Foundation
NixEval:
PackageModel: Foundation
EnvModel: PackageModel, Compiler front end
Nix: NixEval, PackageModel
# Publication is also the `jetpack_publication` package (Jetpack/Publication/package.jet), so jet_cli can
# depend on it without the engine. Package fetch ingests into the Store, so it lives in Engine
# (Provider/Source/PackageFetch).
Publication: PackageModel
# Trust is also the `jetpack_trust` package (Jetpack/Trust/package.jet), which jet_cli uses.
Trust: Compiler front end, Foundation, PackageModel, EnvModel, TrustRoot
# Environment (shell prompt, activation, managed files, locks, overlays, transitions) sits below
# the engine so jet_cli can reach it without the Store; the composed ShellEnv holds Store cache
# leases, so it lives in Store/Source/Shell.
Environment: Compiler front end, Foundation, PackageModel, EnvModel, Trust
# Engine holds Store, Recipe and Provider: their sources use each other in a cycle
# (Store reaches Provider), so they check as one unit until #4589 breaks it.
Engine: PackageModel, TrustRoot, EnvModel, Nix, Publication, Trust, Environment
Secrets: Compiler front end, Foundation, PackageModel, EnvModel, Engine
Services: Engine
Image: Engine
JetOS: EnvModel, Engine
Integrations: Compiler front end, Foundation, PackageModel
CLI: Services, Image, Integrations, Secrets, JetOS
